React component primitive-shape audit & continuous enforcement
## Goal

Every authored React component should match the shape our `@tools/generators` component template emits out of the gate — and stay that way. Audit the codebase, fix the drift, and replace "soft suggestion" with hard, continuous enforcement.

## Canonical shape (source: `tools/generators/src/generators/{react,react-router}/files/component/__name__.tsx` — the two templates are byte-identical)

```tsx
import * as React from 'react';

export interface FooProps {
  // empty interface is fine — presence is the point
}

export const Foo = (_props: FooProps): React.ReactElement => {
  // Hooks

  // Setup

  // Handlers

  // Markup

  // Life Cycle

  // 🔌 Short Circuit

  return ( ... ); // always returns something or null
};
```

Rules that matter:
- **Export both** the component (`Foo`) and its props (`FooProps`) — even an empty interface. We assume it's present in specs; it makes future changes consistent and cheap.
- **Markers are mandatory**: `// Hooks`, `// Setup`, `// Handlers`, `// Markup`, `// Life Cycle`, `// 🔌 Short Circuit`. **Order and whitespace matter** — a blank line where there's no early return; the fixed order prevents Rules-of-Hooks violations and gives controlled places to put code.
- **Hoist non-component code**: utilities that live outside the component go in the sibling `../utils` folder; hardcoded data/config goes in the sibling `../data` folder (copy → `data.copy.ts`). One component per file; keep the section scaffold even when empty.

## Baseline (measured 2026-07-30)
- 507 authored `*.tsx` under `components/` folders (excluding `__tests__`, `__generated__`, `dist`).
- ~83% already carry the markers. Marker *presence* is only a proxy — real conformance (export names, marker **order + whitespace**, utils/data hoisting) requires an AST check.
- Largest "non-conformant" cluster is `react-router-shadcn` (62 files) — **vendored cva/Radix primitives, intentionally out of scope**.
- Real authored tail missing markers: **~22 files** across ~7 projects.

## Decisions (locked with owner)
- **Enforcement = BOTH**: a custom ESLint rule (per-file structural rules — export names, markers, order, whitespace; ships an autofix) **and** a repo-wide ts-morph audit script (cross-file rules ESLint can't see — inline utils/data that belong in `../utils` / `../data`). ESLint gives editor + `nx lint` + CI feedback; the audit script gives the repo-wide inventory and structural checks.
- **Scope = exclude vendored**: `react-router-shadcn`, `dist/**`, `__generated__/**`, `__tests__/**`, plus an explicit per-file opt-out escape hatch. Only our authored components are held to the shape.

## Strategy
Design the spec + scope → build both enforcers in **report-only** mode → generate the true baseline inventory → **land a small pilot PR** on one package (checkpoint: "on the right track?") → bulldoze project-by-project flipping the rule to `error` as each area is cleaned → flip on globally + CI gate + a generator↔rule contract test so the template and enforcer can never drift.

## Non-negotiables
- Enforcement must **block**, not suggest: rule severity `error`, wired into `nx lint` and `check:local`/CI.
- The generator template stays the single source of truth; a contract test asserts generated output passes the rule + audit.
- No breaking changes to shadcn/vendored; no divergence from the upstream shadcn registry.
Bring all authored React components to the generator's primitive shape (exported Foo + FooProps, ordered markers, hoisted utils/data), then enforce it hard and continuously via a custom ESLint rule + a repo-wide AST audit script. Start small with a pilot PR, then bulldoze project-by-project.
